From william.stewart at rbc.com  Wed Apr  5 21:43:10 2006
From: william.stewart at rbc.com (Stewart, William)
Date: Wed, 05 Apr 2006 17:43:10 -0400
Subject: Re: setMethod error
In-Reply-To: <DEC3A9EEFB2768459EF926446352F7CD0CC687@EXNJMB61.nam.nsroot.net>
Message-ID: <BE129D1A0A253A4BA526C4500064850A01636B1C@sew01065.maple.fg.rbc.com>

Andrew,

The following is an admittedly long response to your question, but I
thought it was worth trying to explain why the error was occurring to
help you avoid similar problems in the future. I hope this helps:

The generic signature for "[" is function(x, ..., drop=TRUE). Note that
the generic signature uses "..." to allow for a flexible number of
arguments to be passed.

The first warning message you get is because the signature you are
defining in your setMethod is function(x, ind) which is not exactly the
same as the generic signature (this isn't necessarily a problem in and
of itself, and S-Plus tries to add a second layer {local function} to
accommodate, but it is best to keep your signature consistent with the
generic).

The error you are experiencing arises from the fact that your signature
is being interpreted as follows:

function(x, ind)   **your signature
function(h,1,2)    **once your inputs are passed by calling "h[1:2]".
(Note that the series 1:2 expands to c(1,2) )

Note that "h" corresponds to "x", "1" corresponds to "ind", and "2" is
not matched to any parameter in your signature. This is what is causing
the error you are experiencing.

If you use the generic signature you get the following:

function(x, ..., drop=FALSE)     **generic signature
function(h, 1, 2, drop=FALSE)    **once your inputs are passed by
calling "h[1:2]"

In this case, "h" corresponds to "x", and the series 1:2 is captured in
"..." as the expansion c(1,2).

(Note: the "drop" parameter allows you to simplify the class, if
possible. TRUE simplifies and is the default, FALSE keeps the original
class, regardless, and is probably what you want in this case.)

You now have to handle the "...", which can be confusing if you haven't
used it before, to allow for a variable number of parameters to be
passed to your function.

For what you are trying to accomplish here, you can just pass it
directly as an index to your main code, like this:

history(d=x@dates[...], v=x@values[...])

Which would be interpreted as:

history(d=x@dates[c(1,2)], v=x@values[c(1,2)])   **based on your example

If you were trying to do something more complex, you may need to
identify and handle individual elements of "..."

A couple of useful functions for dealing with "..." are:

my.dots <- list(...)     **my.dots would be a list with each list
element corresponding to an element of "..."

num.dots <- nDotArgs(...)  **num.dots would be the number of elements
passed through "..."

..1 would be the first element of "...", ..2 would be the second element
of "...", etc. Be careful to check the number of elements passed,
however, as if you use ..2 and it doesn't exist (that is, only 1 or 0
elements were passed to "...") you will get an error.

