Karl,

There is an easy solution to your problem if you already have a function that 
generates all combinations of n things taken 2 at a time.
I use the 'combn2' function from the Combinatorics package by Scott D. Chasalow 
(as was posted in StatLib). I am attaching the script for this function at the 
end of this message.
Following this, a solution to your problem is as follows:

> mat
  group     values 
1     1 -0.9082901
2     1 -0.3202220
3     1 -0.4415473
4     1 -1.8012052
5     2 -0.3013117
6     2  0.9849585
7     2 -0.1791473
8     2 -0.3638630

> as.vector(sapply(lapply(split(mat[,2],mat[,1]),FUN=combn2),function(x) 
> apply(x,1,diff)))

[1]  0.58806818  0.46674286 -0.89291502 -0.12132531 -1.48098320 -1.35965788  
1.28627016  0.12216438 -0.06255135 -1.16410578 -1.34882152 -0.18471574
 
HTH

Dimitrios Chalvatzis 
Risk Analyst 
Energy Wholesale/Risk Policies & Methods 
T +44 (0)24 7642 5429 
F +44 (0)24 7642 5479 
Dimitrios.Chalvatzis at eon-uk.com 

######################################
#### Script for 'combn2' function ####

combn2<-function(x, n)
{
        #   DATE WRITTEN:  14 April 1994           LAST REVISED:  14 April 1994
        #   AUTHOR:  Scott D. Chasalow
        #
        #   DESCRIPTION:
        #         Generate all combinations of the elements of x taken two at a 
time. 
        #         If x is missing,  generate all combinations of 1:n taken two
        #         at a time (that is,  the indices of x that would give all 
        #         combinations of the elements of x if x with length n had been 
given).
        #         Exactly one of arguments "x" and "n" should be given.
        #
        if(!missing(x)) {
                n <- length(x)
                if(!missing(n))
                        warning(paste("Only one of arguments x and n allowed;", 
"argument n was ignored"))
        }
        else if(missing(n))
                stop("Arguments \"x\" and \"n\" both missing")
        if(length(n) > 1) {
                warning(paste("Argument n has", length(n), "elements: only the 
first used"))
                n <- n[1]
        }
        if(n == 0)
                return(NULL)
        rmat <- array(seq(length = n), c(n, n))
        # row(matrix(0,n,n))
        cmat <- t(rmat)
        # col(matrix(0,n,n))
        lower.t <- rmat > cmat
        # lower.tri(matrix(0,n,n))
        i1 <- cmat[lower.t]
        i2 <- rmat[lower.t]
        if(missing(x))
                cbind(i1, i2)
        else cbind(x[i1], x[i2])
}
