Maybe the following very simple and aesthetic code is what you are looking for:

____________________________________________________________________

my.diff <- function(x)  

# Computes all pairwise differences of elements of a vector x

{outer(x,x,"-")}

my.sub <- function(X)

# Extracts lower triangle from matrix X

{X[row(X) > col(X)]}

# This command does the job

-unlist(lapply(tapply(A[,2], A[,1], my.diff), my.sub))

____________________________________________________________________

An Example Run:

> # Generate some Test Data with unequal number of elements per group

> A <- cbind(rep(1:2,c(3,5)), sample(1:8))

> colnames(A) <- c("Group", "Values")

> A

     Group Values

[1,]     1      6

[2,]     1      7

[3,]     1      5

[4,]     2      1

[5,]     2      4

[6,]     2      3

[7,]     2      2

[8,]     2      8

 

> -unlist(lapply(tapply(A[,2], A[,1], my.diff), my.sub))

 11  12  13  21  22  23  24  25  26  27  28  29 210 

 -1   1   2  -3  -2  -1  -7   1   2  -4   1  -5  -6

Please note that the names give you an indication of the semantic of the elements of the resulting vector. The first digit indicates the group, while the remaining digits serve as an index which allows determination of the specific difference represented.

Of course a more compact notation is possible but due to reasons of readability I prefer the explicit definition of the two functions involved.

With best regards

Marcus Hudec

